# Early adb: bring up the USB gadget for adb from init, so a hung or slow
# system_server cannot block device diagnosis (USB config in a normal boot
# is driven by system_server UsbService late in boot).
# The device usb rc keys everything off sys.usb.config=adb (starts adbd +
# wires the UDC); this trigger fires as soon as `on boot` sets configfs=2.
# Unconditional: bring-up builds must always be diagnosable.
on property:sys.usb.configfs=2
    setprop sys.usb.config adb

# Key-store layout. adbd runs as uid/gid `shell` on a secure build, so it can
# only read /data/misc/adb/adb_keys when the file's group is `shell`. The
# framework creates that file 0640 and relies on the directory being setgid
# `system:shell` -- the AOSP `mkdir /data/misc/adb 02750 system shell` in
# init.usb.rc -- for the group to come out right.
#
# This action used to `mkdir ... 0755 system system` and then `write` a
# hardcoded build-host key over adb_keys. That pair is why the device "forgot
# this PC": every boot replaced the approved-key store with one developer key
# (mode 0644), and the framework later rewrote it 0640 system:system inside a
# directory adbd could not traverse as `shell`, so the host had to be
# re-approved after every reboot. Never write adb_keys from init -- it belongs
# to the framework's AdbDebuggingManager.
#
# mkdir is a no-op when the directory exists, so an already-wrong directory
# from an earlier build is repaired explicitly. On a freshly formatted /data
# these chmod/chown commands fail harmlessly and init.usb.rc's mkdir creates
# the directory with the correct mode and owner.
#
# Owners are numeric (system=1000, shell=2000) because name resolution failed
# for `shell` on this build's toybox; init accepts either form.
on property:sys.usb.configfs=2
    mkdir /data/misc/adb 02750 1000 2000
    chown 1000 2000 /data/misc/adb
    chmod 02750 /data/misc/adb
    chown 1000 2000 /data/misc/adb/adb_keys
    chmod 0640 /data/misc/adb/adb_keys
    restorecon -R /data/misc/adb
